### Kernel Density Estimation Experiment

The purpose of this activity is to "stress-test" the Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) method. While standard kernels (like the Gaussian or Rectangular) are designed to center probability mass around the observed data, we will deliberately design a counter-intuitive kernel. Our custom kernel, $K_C(u) = \vert{}u\vert{}$, pushes the density away from the observation point and towards the edges of its support. By doing this, we aim to understand the mechanical role of kernel shape and to demonstrate that mathematical validity does not automatically guarantee a meaningful visual summary.


```{r echo=FALSE}
blue   <- "#0072B280"
orange <- "#D55E0075"
green  <- "#009E73"

kde <- function(x, grid, h) {
  rowMeans(dnorm(outer(grid, x, "-") / h)) / h
}
trapz <- function(x, y) sum(diff(x) * (head(y, -1) + tail(y, -1)) / 2)
```


