#### Bandwidth Sensitivity

Changing the bandwidth does not fix the structural flaw of the custom kernel. With a halved bandwidth (*under-smoothing*), the custom kernel amplifies its weird behavior, turning the curve into a chaotic sequence of false peaks and valleys. With a doubled bandwidth (*over-smoothing*), the central valley widens, and the mass is unnaturally pushed toward the tails of the distribution. Since changing $h$ only scales the distance at which probability is pushed away from the data, a different bandwidth cannot save a bad kernel.

#### Final Conclusion

A visually misleading plot does not mean the custom kernel is mathematically invalid. Our $K_C(u)$ strictly respects all formal requirements: it is symmetric, non-negative, and its area integrates to 1. However, this experiment shows that a mathematically correct kernel can still produce a useless statistical summary. This happens when its shape violates the core intuition of density estimation: the estimated probability should be highest near the actual observations, and decrease as we move away from them.
